  "prediction": "**Department of Defense's Efforts to Address Unwanted Sexual Behaviors**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has defined various types of unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. The DOD has established policies and procedures to address these behaviors, but a recent review by the Government Accountability Office (GAO) found that some of these policies are incomplete or inconsistent.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **Incomplete Policies:** The GAO found that DOD's policies on sexual harassment do not fully include the principles of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence.\n2. **Lack of Consistency:** The GAO found that DOD's policies on sexual harassment vary across different branches of the military, with some branches having more comprehensive policies than others.\n3. **Data Collection:** The GAO found that DOD's data collection on unwanted sexual behaviors is inconsistent and incomplete, making it difficult to track and address these behaviors.\n4. **Collaborative Efforts:** The GAO found that DOD has established collaborative efforts to address unwanted sexual behaviors, but these efforts are not always effective.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Incorporate CDC Principles:** The GAO recommends that DOD incorporate the principles of the CDC's framework for preventing sexual violence into its policies on sexual harassment.\n2. **Standardize Policies:** The GAO recommends that DOD standardize its policies on sexual harassment across all branches of the military.\n3. **Improve Data Collection:** The GAO recommends that DOD improve its data collection on unwanted sexual behaviors to better track and address these behaviors.\n4. **Enhance Collaborative Efforts:** The GAO recommends that DOD enhance its collaborative efforts to address unwanted sexual behaviors.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe DOD has made efforts to address unwanted sexual behaviors, but more work is needed to ensure that its policies and procedures are comprehensive and effective. By incorporating the principles of the CDC's framework for preventing sexual violence, standardizing policies, improving data collection, and enhancing collaborative efforts, the DOD can better address these behaviors and create a safer and more respectful environment for all servicemembers.",
